  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Rome's most notorious party town is now under the sea. Baiae, on the Bay of Naples. Its mosaic floors sit about five metres down.",
        "visual_query": "bay of naples rocky coastline",
        "visual_fallback": "italian coastline"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Seneca called it a harbour of vice. Caesar kept a villa here. Nero built one. Hadrian died in his.",
        "visual_query": "roman villa ruins italy sunlight",
        "visual_fallback": "roman ruins"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The whole town was built on hot mineral springs, and one bath dome was the widest in the world before the Pantheon. So what sank it?",
        "visual_query": "ancient roman bath dome interior",
        "visual_fallback": "roman bath"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Not a wave, not an earthquake. The ground itself. The volcanic field underneath breathes, lifting and dropping this coast by metres, and Baiae sat on the part that sank.",
        "visual_query": "campi flegrei volcanic steam vents",
        "visual_fallback": "volcanic vents"
      },
      {
        "narration": "By the eighth century the lower town was sea floor. Since 2002 it is a protected park, and divers swim its streets past the emperors' floors.",
        "visual_query": "underwater roman ruins scuba diver",
        "visual_fallback": "underwater ruins"
      }
    ],
