      {
        "claim": "Seneca called Baiae a harbour of vice; Julius Caesar and Nero had villas there and Hadrian died at his villa there in AD 138.",
        "segment_index": 1,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "100% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article, including 1 number(s)",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "Baiae was built around warm mineral springs, and the structure once called the Temple of Mercury is part of a thermal bath complex with a dome about 21.5 metres across, the largest in the world before the Pantheon.",
        "segment_index": 2,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "91% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article, including 2 number(s)",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "The submersion of Baiae was caused by bradyseism, ground movement from volcanic activity in the Phlegraean Fields, which lowered the coastline in phases.",
